    "question": {
      ":question/id": "79177c88-8d02-5409-a8a5-b25ab8039bee",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x)=(x^{4} - 4 x)^{2}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{2},\\,g=x^{4} - 4 x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4 x^{3} - 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=2g\\cdot (4 x^{3} - 4) \\\\  &=2(x^{4} - 4 x)(4 x^{3} - 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
